Sources/GT10Ring.c	undated	Measured 2026-09-17 on this Apple Silicon Mac. A plain store can become visible to another core before an EARLIER atomic store to a different address (about 700k violations in 400M reads, lag up to 60 stores). Plain-then-atomic and atomic-then-atomic showed 0 violations in 2.8B reads. So never announce a write atomically and then copy data expecting a reader to see the announcement. The ring is lock free anyway: the writer publishes end after copying, the reader raises a floor before copying, and the writer never stores beyond floor + capacity, so every stale value is stale in the safe direction. Removing that floor guard makes the concurrent test fail.
